import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a0 as n}from"./chunks/framework.9TNW7Bv-.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Patch bay: mount an app, point a domain at it, change it, disconnect it","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"PatchBay.md","filePath":"PatchBay.md"}'),d={name:"PatchBay.md"};function s(i,e,r,h,c,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="patch-bay-mount-an-app-point-a-domain-at-it-change-it-disconnect-it" tabindex="-1">Patch bay: mount an app, point a domain at it, change it, disconnect it <a class="header-anchor" href="#patch-bay-mount-an-app-point-a-domain-at-it-change-it-disconnect-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Patch bay: mount an app, point a domain at it, change it, disconnect it&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Status: built and verified on a disposable gateway (2026-10-12), panel included. Not activated on the machine&#39;s gateway.</strong> The journey is <code>tests/patch-bay-journey.test.ts</code>; the panel&#39;s behaviour was driven in a browser against a disposable backend.</p><h2 id="two-routing-planes-they-do-not-have-the-same-capabilities" tabindex="-1">Two routing planes (they do not have the same capabilities) <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-routing-planes-they-do-not-have-the-same-capabilities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two routing planes (they do not have the same capabilities)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Plane A: <code>conf.d/netget_app.conf</code></th><th>Plane B: the main <code>nginx.conf</code> default server</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Serves</td><td><code>&lt;machine&gt;.local</code>, <code>&lt;handle&gt;.&lt;machine&gt;.local</code>, <code>localhost</code>, <code>/apps/&lt;name&gt;/</code></td><td>any other domain in <code>domain-map.json</code></td></tr><tr><td>Decides with</td><td><code>surface_proxy.lua</code> / <code>monad_proxy.lua</code></td><td>an inline Lua router (<code>setNginxConfigFile.ts</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>app:&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>by bare name, <code>monad:&lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>metadata.monadName</code>, live by TTL</td><td>(fixed 2026-10-12) same rules</td></tr><tr><td>Per-prefix connections (<code>door-connections</code>)</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>no</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Exposure policy / authorization of the app</td><td>yes</td><td><strong>no</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Errors</td><td>JSON (connections) / explanatory pages</td><td>explanatory pages (503 service, 404 not found)</td></tr><tr><td>Needs TLS</td><td>no</td><td><strong>yes</strong>: the route must carry a certificate (see below)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-works-verified" tabindex="-1">What works (verified) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-works-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What works (verified)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Static app: <code>type: static</code>, <code>target: &lt;absolute folder&gt;</code>. Service on a port: <code>type: server</code>, <code>target: 127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>. A registered monad: <code>type: server</code>, <code>target: app:&lt;name&gt;</code>.</p><ol><li><code>POST /add-domain</code> registers the door; <code>domain-map.json</code> is regenerated and the router picks it up within a second.</li><li>The domain opens (files, or the service, with the visitor&#39;s Host and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>).</li><li><code>POST /update-domain</code> changes <code>target</code> and/or <code>type</code> of the same record: service A -&gt; service B, files A -&gt; files B, service &lt;-&gt; files. Nothing is created or copied.</li><li>The service goes away: the visitor gets the gateway&#39;s 503 page, naming what the domain is pointed at; other domains are unaffected.</li><li><code>POST /delete-domain</code> disconnects the door; the app&#39;s files and processes are not touched.</li><li>Authorization: an anonymous caller gets 401 <code>ADMIN_SESSION_REQUIRED</code>, a session without <code>gateway:write</code> gets 403 <code>CAPABILITY_DENIED</code>, a session with it succeeds; listing <code>GET /domains</code> is public and trimmed.</li></ol><h2 id="supported-destinations-declared-src-runtime-routetargets-ts-is-the-one-definition" tabindex="-1">Supported destinations (declared; <code>src/runtime/routeTargets.ts</code> is the one definition) <a class="header-anchor" href="#supported-destinations-declared-src-runtime-routetargets-ts-is-the-one-definition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Supported destinations (declared; `src/runtime/routeTargets.ts` is the one definition)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Route</th><th>Target</th><th>Protections</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>App registered with this gateway</td><td><code>server</code> / <code>proxy</code></td><td><code>app:&lt;name&gt;</code></td><td>the app&#39;s exposure policy and guest trust apply to the domain too; the app&#39;s own writes are authorized by the app</td></tr><tr><td>Service at a port</td><td><code>server</code></td><td><code>host:port</code> (this machine or another one; <code>http://</code> is normalized away; a bare port means <code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>)</td><td>refused: gateway ports (80, 443, 3000) on a loopback name, the port of a registered app (reach it by <code>app:</code>), link-local <code>169.254.x.x</code>, <code>https://</code> upstreams (not supported yet), a path or credentials in the target</td></tr><tr><td>Folder of files</td><td><code>static</code></td><td>absolute path (no <code>..</code>)</td><td>the panel says when it does not exist or has no <code>index.html</code></td></tr><tr><td>The monad&#39;s surface</td><td><code>proxy</code></td><td>none</td><td>resolved by the monad</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The same rules are enforced by the store (every writer: the panel, <code>/add-domain</code>, <code>/update-domain</code>, <code>netget routing</code>) and again when the map is projected for the router, so no entry is a way around another. What the router hands a service: the real peer in <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> (replaced, not appended) and none of <code>X-NetGet-Identity</code>, <code>X-Netget-Scopes</code>, <code>X-NetGet-Internal</code>, <code>X-Me-Proof</code>, <code>X-Monad-Internal-Token</code>.</p><h2 id="what-the-panel-does-now-domains-jsx" tabindex="-1">What the panel does now (<code>Domains.jsx</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-panel-does-now-domains-jsx" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the panel does now (`Domains.jsx`)&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Conectar puerta</strong>: domain + destination by kind (a registered app from the live registry, a service, a folder, the monad&#39;s surface). A refusal from the gateway is shown with its reason.</li><li><strong>Cambiar destino</strong>: the same record, a new destination (&quot;no se crea otra identidad ni se copian datos&quot;).</li><li><strong>Estado</strong> per door, from <code>GET /domains/status</code> (admin): the destination answers / does not / is missing / unknown; applied to the router or not; TLS configured or pending. When the gateway cannot answer the panel says &quot;estado desconocido&quot; and lists the routes anyway.</li><li><strong>Abrir</strong>: enabled only when the door has a certificate; otherwise the tooltip says &quot;TLS pendiente&quot;. The panel does not hide the failure behind a wrong certificate: without one the router cannot complete an HTTPS handshake at all.</li><li><strong>Desconectar</strong>: removes the record; the dialog says that files and the app are not touched, and the notice repeats it.</li></ul><h2 id="what-is-missing" tabindex="-1">What is missing <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-missing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is missing&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A certificate is still the operator&#39;s act.</strong> The add form has no certificate fields; <code>provision-cert</code> needs public DNS and ACME, so locally a door is &quot;TLS pendiente&quot; unless a certificate is assigned through the API.</li><li><strong>Port 80</strong>: a registered domain is neither served nor redirected over plain HTTP; the default server answers with the gateway&#39;s own page whatever the host is.</li><li><strong>Door connections (prefix -&gt; service) on the domain router</strong> and the <strong>authorization the app declared for operations</strong> (<code>requiredForDestructive</code>) are plane A only; plane B applies the app&#39;s exposure and trust, not per-operation rules. Unifying both planes behind one decision is not done.</li><li><strong>A connections editor in the panel</strong> (CLI only), and a <strong>per-door &quot;why it failed&quot; history</strong>.</li><li><strong>Other gateways</strong> (below).</li></ul><h2 id="gateway-selector-the-panel-looks-at-one-gateway-at-a-time-and-says-which" tabindex="-1">Gateway selector (the panel looks at one gateway at a time, and says which) <a class="header-anchor" href="#gateway-selector-the-panel-looks-at-one-gateway-at-a-time-and-says-which" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Gateway selector (the panel looks at one gateway at a time, and says which)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Domains panel has a gateway bar: &quot;Este netget&quot; (the one that serves the panel) plus the gateways the operator adds by an <strong>explicit address</strong> (<code>netget.site</code>, <code>raspberrypi.local:3000</code>, <code>http://192.168.1.40</code>). Nothing is discovered and nothing is added by itself; the list lives in this browser (<code>localStorage</code>). The bar says which gateway is being viewed and, for it:</p><ul><li><strong>what can be queried</strong>: its four public reads (<code>/gateway-identity</code>, <code>/healthcheck</code>, <code>/main-server-namespace</code>, <code>/domains</code>), each tried and classified (responds / refused / unreachable / not JSON). A browser cannot tell &quot;nothing is there&quot; from &quot;CORS refused it&quot;; the panel says so instead of choosing;</li><li><strong>what may be modified</strong>: asked of that gateway with the session stored FOR it, by <code>POST /add-domain {}</code> -- the capability gate answers before the handler looks at the body (401 not accepted, 403 valid but no <code>gateway:write</code>, 400 may: the empty request was refused, nothing written). Without a session the panel does not ask and says &quot;solo lo que divulga&quot;;</li><li><strong>whether its configuration is readable</strong>: <code>/domains</code> with the session returning destinations. A gateway with no doors has nothing to check this against and is &quot;Desconocido&quot;, not &quot;Sí&quot;.</li></ul><p>A session is stored under the gateway&#39;s own origin and only ever sent there. The existing endpoints and authorization are reused as they are: no new route, no new scope. Writes (connect, change destination, disconnect) go to the selected gateway with its session; the button is disabled while the gateway has not authorized writes. After a write the summary asks again, and notices from one gateway are dropped when another is selected.</p><p>Walked in the Browser pane against a disposable second gateway B (CORS on, tokens <code>b-reader</code>, <code>b-admin</code>) and the real <code>https://netget.site</code> without a session:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>gateway / session</th><th>reads</th><th>modify</th><th>observed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>local, session</td><td>4 respond</td><td>yes</td><td>empty request refused after authorization</td></tr><tr><td>B, none</td><td>4 respond</td><td>no</td><td>&quot;Conectar puerta&quot; disabled</td></tr><tr><td>B, token of another gateway</td><td>4 respond</td><td>no</td><td>401 &quot;no acepta esta sesión&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>B, <code>b-reader</code></td><td>4 respond</td><td>no</td><td>403 &quot;solo lectura&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>B, <code>b-admin</code></td><td>4 respond</td><td>yes</td><td>door connected from the panel exists on B and not on local; disconnected the same way</td></tr><tr><td><code>netget.site</code>, none</td><td>4 respond</td><td>no</td><td>identity, namespace <code>cleaker.me</code>, v2.56, 4 public doors; <code>/domains/status</code> is 404 there, so the panel lists the routes and says the state is unknown</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="with-the-real-authorization-and-with-answers-that-arrive-late-2026-10-13" tabindex="-1">With the real authorization, and with answers that arrive late (2026-10-13) <a class="header-anchor" href="#with-the-real-authorization-and-with-answers-that-arrive-late-2026-10-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;With the real authorization, and with answers that arrive late (2026-10-13)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The table above used a test gate. The same walk was repeated against two disposable gateways that run the real gate (<code>tests/gateway-admin-session-gate.test.ts</code>&#39;s setup: a monad with the gateway module, an owner and a read-only admin that sign in with a keychain key -&gt; challenge -&gt; session; CORS in front, an optional delay per request):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>session on that gateway</th><th>reads</th><th>its configuration</th><th>modify</th><th>observed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>none</td><td>4 respond</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>&quot;solo lo que divulga&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>read-only admin</td><td>4 respond</td><td><strong>Sí</strong> (targets)</td><td>No</td><td>403 &quot;solo lectura&quot;; &quot;Conectar puerta&quot; disabled; row states stay &quot;desconocido&quot; (<code>/domains/status</code> is gated)</td></tr><tr><td>the same admin after <code>gateway:write</code> is granted</td><td>4 respond</td><td>Sí</td><td><strong>Sí</strong></td><td>same token, re-asked: the button enables</td></tr><tr><td>the same admin after the grant is taken back</td><td>4 respond</td><td>Sí</td><td>No</td><td>403 again, button disabled</td></tr><tr><td>owner</td><td>4 respond</td><td>Sí</td><td>Sí</td><td>a door was connected from the panel; it shows &quot;TLS pendiente / Responde&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Answers that arrive late. Every asynchronous answer (probe, route list, states, apps, and a write already sent) is applied only while the gateway AND session it was asked under are the ones on screen (<code>scopeKey</code>). Walked with a gateway that answers in 4-5 s:</p><ul><li><strong>Before the guard</strong> (control, the previous build): selecting the slow gateway and then &quot;Este netget&quot; before it answered left the slow gateway&#39;s route (<code>puerta-slow.test</code>) and a probe report under the &quot;Este netget&quot; header.</li><li><strong>With the guard</strong>: the same sequence left only &quot;Este netget&quot;&#39;s own data for the 20 s observed.</li><li><strong>An operation already sent keeps its destination</strong>: &quot;Desconectar&quot; on the slow gateway, then switching to local before it answered: the door was removed on the slow gateway (checked there), the local table did not change, and the notice names the gateway (&quot;... desconectado en Real lento.&quot;). An add cannot be switched away from: its dialog is modal.</li></ul><p>Not walked: a session that changes while its requests are in flight (covered only by the scope key&#39;s unit test), and a key revoked under a live session (covered by <code>gateway-admin-session-gate.test.ts</code>, not through the panel).</p><p>Limits: modifying <code>netget.site</code> from here was <strong>not</strong> done: it needs the VM&#39;s own admin session and an explicit go. The panel&#39;s refresh reloads routes and states as well as the permission summary. The mesh (discovery, bridges between gateways) is separate and not part of the selector.</p><h2 id="authorization-is-applied-where-the-write-is-executed-2026-10-13" tabindex="-1">Authorization is applied where the write is executed (2026-10-13) <a class="header-anchor" href="#authorization-is-applied-where-the-write-is-executed-2026-10-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authorization is applied where the write is executed (2026-10-13)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The panel shows what a session may do (reads, &quot;Modificar puertas&quot;, a disabled &quot;Conectar puerta&quot;). That is only a representation; the decision has to be taken by the process that executes the write.</p><p><strong>Finding.</strong> The standalone backend (<code>src/htmls/Netget-REACT/backend/proxy.js</code>, what <code>netget backend-start</code> runs on <code>:3000</code>) mounted the gateway&#39;s routers with no gate (&quot;loopback only, nginx&#39;s job&quot;). Anything that could reach <code>127.0.0.1:3000</code> could add, change or delete a route: a panel run against it created a route on the machine&#39;s real store with a bearer that was not a session. The gate (<code>gateway/adminGate.mjs</code>) existed only where the routes are mounted behind a monad (<code>gateway/monadModule.mjs</code>, as on the VM).</p><p><strong>Now.</strong> <code>proxy.js</code> mounts its routers through the same <code>mountGatewayRouters</code> (origin guard + gate), with the same <code>resolveAdminSession</code> and <code>GatewayClaimsManager.isOwner</code>. Same three classes of route:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>class</th><th>routes</th><th>who</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>public</td><td>the reads in <code>PUBLIC_ROUTES</code> (identity, namespace, <code>GET /domains</code> -&gt; allow-listed view, health, ...) and, new, <code>POST /explain</code>, <code>POST /inspect</code> (forwarded to the monad&#39;s own public endpoints)</td><td>anyone</td></tr><tr><td>own</td><td><code>/setup/*</code>, <code>/admin-session/*</code>, <code>/gateway-admin/*</code>, <code>GET /logs</code>, and, new, <code>/openresty/install*</code> (its own <code>requireAuth</code>)</td><td>what the route itself verifies</td></tr><tr><td>trusted</td><td>everything else: <code>add/update/delete-domain</code>, <code>provision-cert</code>, <code>domains/metadata</code>, <code>domains/status</code>, `openresty-restart</td><td>stop<code>, </code>frontend-mode<code>, </code>__gateway/claim<code>, </code>apps/report`</td></tr></tbody></table><p>What it means for a client: no session -&gt; 401 <code>ADMIN_SESSION_REQUIRED</code>; a session without <code>gateway:write</code> -&gt; 403 <code>CAPABILITY_DENIED</code> naming <code>gateway:write</code>; identity headers (<code>x-netget-identity</code> / <code>x-netget-scopes</code>) are set from the session and any the request carried are discarded. A read-only admin still reads the configuration (<code>GET /domains</code> with its bearer). <code>gateway:write:domain-metadata</code> stays a second check inside <code>/domains/metadata</code>, on top of the gate.</p><p><strong>Not the same as before.</strong> (1) The panel needs a stored session to enable writes (sign-in is the Logs page / the challenge-verify flow); a bare <code>curl</code> to <code>:3000</code> no longer writes. (2) The identity headers that nginx&#39;s Lua perimeter set for a signed proof are honored only from an internal caller, as on the VM. (3) The monad-only parts are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Tests.</strong> <code>tests/standalone-backend-gate.test.ts</code> starts that entry point itself (a child process, <code>tests/helpers/standaloneBackend.ts</code>) on a temp data dir, with a monad of its own and a marker namespace, and refuses to send any write until <code>GET /main-server-namespace</code> has answered this run&#39;s marker. It checks anonymous refusal (with forged headers and a bogus bearer), the public reads, a read-only admin, an owner&#39;s connect -&gt; change destination -&gt; disconnect (and a destination the validation refuses), a grant read fresh on every call and taken back, the second capability, and that none of the machine&#39;s own gateway files was written. Run against the previous <code>proxy.js</code> it fails at the first anonymous add (200 instead of 401). <code>gateway-capability-model.test.ts</code> is a LIVE test of the real gateway and is now opt-in (<code>NETGET_LIVE_GATEWAY_TEST=1</code>).</p><p>The panel was walked against that entry (disposable data, a marker proven through the dev proxy before the first write): no session (summary &quot;No&quot;, button disabled), a read-only session (reads the configuration, 403 on a hand-sent delete, the route survived), the owner (connect -&gt; change destination -&gt; disconnect from the panel, states and notices as shown).</p><h3 id="with-nginx-in-front-and-what-the-tests-leave-behind-2026-10-13" tabindex="-1">With nginx in front, and what the tests leave behind (2026-10-13) <a class="header-anchor" href="#with-nginx-in-front-and-what-the-tests-leave-behind-2026-10-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;With nginx in front, and what the tests leave behind (2026-10-13)&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>tests/nginx-fronted-gate.test.ts</code> puts a disposable OpenResty (generated main and app conf, or with <code>NGINX_TEST_INSTALLED=1</code> the machine&#39;s INSTALLED <code>conf.d/netget_app.conf</code> and Lua tree, read only) in front of the real <code>proxy.js</code> and walks the operator&#39;s path through it: anonymous and forged-header refusals (<code>X-NetGet-Identity</code>, <code>X-NetGet-Scopes</code>, <code>X-Monad-Internal-Token</code>, peer-address headers; loopback is not trust), sessions obtained by the existing challenge/verify flow through nginx, an administrator let in and a read-only one refused naming the capability, connect -&gt; change -&gt; disconnect, the front end served, the loaded configuration naming no missing directory and its error log holding only lua-resty-http missing (a one-off public-IP timer, the same on the machine&#39;s own log). Red against the previous <code>proxy.js</code>. The panel was also walked in a browser through that nginx (owner, read-only, forged headers).</p><p><code>monad-statedir-lock.test.ts</code> left a monad per run (two were started under one name; the record names one pid). It now finds what it started by its environment and stops it, and <code>monad-statedir-lock-hygiene.test.ts</code> runs it passing and with a failure injected and requires no process and no new listener afterwards.</p><h2 id="what-each-piece-changes-when-it-is-activated-on-this-machine" tabindex="-1">What each piece changes when it is activated on this machine <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-each-piece-changes-when-it-is-activated-on-this-machine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What each piece changes when it is activated on this machine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Four things were being treated as one package; they are not, and none of them is activated yet:</p><ol start="0"><li><strong>The backend of this branch</strong> (<code>proxy.js</code> + routes) -- runs only when the real backend is restarted from a release that carries it (see <code>CHANGES-r20261013-1.md</code>). It is what applies the gate (section above), and the panel&#39;s &quot;Conectar&quot; is only as honest as the backend it talks to. The selector below must not go in front of the backend that is running now: against it the controls would be drawn without anything enforcing them.</li><li><strong>The selector (panel)</strong> -- a static front-end build. Changes: the files nginx serves as the netget page (add-only hashed assets + <code>index.html</code>, with a backup and a rollback). No nginx reload, no Lua, no conf, no store. Against today&#39;s running backend (the older checkout) the selector and the reads work; &quot;Cambiar destino&quot; and the per-door state need the backend routes of this branch (<code>/domains/status</code>, <code>/update-domain</code>), which are 404 there and show as &quot;desconocido&quot; / an error, not as invented state.</li><li><strong>The main <code>nginx.conf</code> repair</strong> -- already written to the file (backup in <code>~/.get/backups/nginx-main-conf-*</code>): only the data-dir and template paths that a test had replaced. The running master still holds what it loaded; it takes effect on the next reload (see &quot;The configuration nginx has LOADED&quot;). It is independent of 1 and 3.</li><li><strong>Door connections</strong> -- <code>conf.d/netget_app.conf</code>, two Lua handlers and one library, plus the main conf, with <code>prepare.sh</code> / <code>activate.sh</code> / <code>rollback.sh</code> (rehearsed byte-identical). Needs a reload. Only this one changes how requests are routed.</li></ol><p>The backend (0) is the one that must come first; none of the four activates another.</p><h2 id="seeing-other-gateways-netget-site-a-raspberry-from-this-panel-what-exists-and-what-does-not" tabindex="-1">Seeing other gateways (netget.site, a Raspberry) from this panel: what exists and what does not <a class="header-anchor" href="#seeing-other-gateways-netget-site-a-raspberry-from-this-panel-what-exists-and-what-does-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Seeing other gateways (netget.site, a Raspberry) from this panel: what exists and what does not&quot;">​</a></h2><p><em>Read</em> works today for what a gateway publishes: <code>https://netget.site/gateway-identity</code>, <code>/domains</code> (public projection: domain, type, tlsConfigured), <code>/main-server-namespace</code> and <code>/healthcheck</code> answer anonymously with <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>, so a local panel can show the public face of the VM or of a Raspberry running netget. <code>/apps</code> is local-only (403 from elsewhere), which is right.</p><p><em>Bridging</em> (a local door whose destination is an app on another gateway) is a different capability and is <strong>not built</strong>:</p><ul><li>the destination needs a kind of its own (<code>gateway:&lt;id&gt;</code> + the app there), declared by the operator in the signed claim record, never learned from a report;</li><li>the path is only as trustworthy as the registration behind it: today&#39;s registry is loopback-only and heartbeats are not signed (CLAUDE.md gaps #1 and #2: surface identity unclaimed, trust ranking not verification), which the cross-host work is gated on;</li><li>&quot;while a path exists&quot; is health: each hop&#39;s answer, from the same status model, with a time bound and a stated unknown;</li><li>&quot;patched by netget&quot; means the operator&#39;s declaration decides the route, as it does for a local door.</li></ul><h2 id="the-configuration-nginx-has-loaded-checked-2026-10-13" tabindex="-1">The configuration nginx has LOADED (checked 2026-10-13) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-configuration-nginx-has-loaded-checked-2026-10-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The configuration nginx has LOADED (checked 2026-10-13)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The master&#39;s pid and start time do not say what it runs: a reload keeps the master and replaces the workers. What does: the live worker (started 2026-10-11 21:32:16, after the last reload) was still opening <code>/var/folders/.../netget-nginxsync-data-GpPdmz/html/NetgetErrorCodeHandler.html</code> on 2026-10-13 15:09 -- a temp dir of a test that had rewritten <code>nginx.conf</code> on 2026-10-11. So the LOADED configuration is the polluted one of 2026-10-11, not the file repaired on 2026-10-12 22:23 (which has <code>~/.get/html</code>). Until <code>sudo openresty -t &amp;&amp; sudo openresty -s reload</code>, the loaded <code>MAP_PATH</code>/templates/html root point at a directory that no longer exists. To check it again without reading memory: the worker&#39;s start time against the file&#39;s mtime, and which data directory the worker&#39;s own error lines name.</p><h2 id="a-finding-about-the-installed-gateway-2026-10-12" tabindex="-1">A finding about the installed gateway (2026-10-12) <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-finding-about-the-installed-gateway-2026-10-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A finding about the installed gateway (2026-10-12)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>tests/nginx-conf-sync.test.ts</code> deleted and rewrote the machine&#39;s real <code>/opt/homebrew/etc/openresty/nginx.conf</code> with a temp data dir. A real nginx error log shows the effect on 2026-10-08 and 2026-10-11, and it happened again during a regression run on 2026-10-12: the main config then reads <code>domain-map.json</code>, the status-page templates and the html root from a temp dir, so plane B cannot route anything on that machine. The test now uses a sandbox layout and asserts the installed file is untouched; <code>~/.get/local/nginx-main-conf/repair.sh</code> renders the correct file for the machine (release templates, real data dir), refuses if anything else differs, backs up, verifies with <code>nginx -t</code> and does not reload.</p>',51)])])}const u=t(d,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
